  "note": "Run on 2026-10-17 on the live AwwO, in the operator’s own account and workspace, on Claude Opus 4.8, every agent working in its own sandbox computer. The first run of the canvas finished all 14 nodes, but in Chrome its page’s opening training failed, so the canvas text was rewritten and the canvas ran 23 more times, each run re-running only the nodes it named (and, unless it said otherwise, what they feed) and keeping the rest. In runs 2 to 7 the lab builder twice and the training builder twice used up their 64 model calls, one builder stopped on a runtime failure, and the pages that came out opened on errors or on training that never started. From run 7 on, each page was opened in Chrome before the next run, and the text of every builder whose part showed wrong was rewritten: in the ninth run, from the spec on, the kernel contract that gives every screen to the lab module, names how a screen is opened, lists a run when it is made and fixes the opening state; then the datasets’ defaults and the spiral, the schedule, the labels and the history, the charts, the status bar, the self-test’s speed, and for the char model the window embedding, its vocabulary, the dense layers’ parameters and the text dataset’s interface. The tensor module ran again in the tenth run and came out worse (backward as a function, not the method the contract names), so it went back to its ninth-run delivery and the text that made it. The page published after the 16th run kept the Data and Train screens as they were first drawn; for the fix, the lab builder came out worse in the 17th run and used up its model calls in the 18th, so the lab kept its 16th-run delivery and the Interface builder, which owns the stage and the dock, took it in the 19th to 21st runs (in the 19th the Train screen’s charts stayed empty, in the 20th the dock did not follow). The 22nd run assembled and tested the page; the tester ran again in the 23rd and 24th, the last with one line about float32 losses in gradient checks added to its task, and fixed two failures: the lab used another name for the text dataset, and the gradient check read the loss in float32. The spec says the opening model has 1,283 parameters; it has 1,251, as the page and its tests count. Each node shows its last run, and every node’s text is the canvas a member copies today. The delivered page is the tester’s, exactly as delivered, with no manual edits.",
  "verification": "Before publishing, the delivered page was opened in Chrome (1440×900 and 1280×720) with every network request blocked: it opens on the Train screen training the Spiral net on the 3-arm spiral, whose decision boundary separates the arms within about two seconds while the loss and accuracy charts and the confusion matrix move. Its self-test passes all 67 tests and rejects all 18 planted failures in about 3 s, as the tester reported. Every screen (Data, Model, Train, Runs, Text, Inside, Self-test) draws with no script error, and each of the nine datasets trains. Picking another dataset redraws the Data screen and the dock at once, and a run started with the Train screen’s New run shows there (checked with circles picked on the Data screen and with moons picked in the dock), while a slider being dragged keeps working. The char model, picked and started from the page, trains on the text and samples in its characters. One fault was seen: Sample with another dataset picked prints nothing and reports an error to the console."
